B2='<p style="margin-top:10px"><span class="link" data-go="b-cause">Open the full answer: Prediction vs causation →</span></p>'
CAUSE=("We test whether environmental information <b>improves forecast accuracy</b>. We compare a model with only past landings and seasonality against one with mangrove history and typhoon exposure added, both evaluated on <b>unseen later quarters</b>. "
"Better accuracy means <b>predictive usefulness only</b>, because fishing effort, fuel prices, regulations and reporting changes can move together with mangrove loss. "
"Proving cause would need controlled or field studies, which are outside our scope. Our results will say \u201cimproves forecast accuracy\u201d, never \u201ccauses\u201d.")
QA={
"title":[
("What is municipal fish landings?","<ul><li>The amount of fish caught at sea by <b>small-scale fishers</b> and brought ashore. They fish without boats or with small boats of <b>three gross tons or less</b>.</li><li>\u2018Landing\u2019 means bringing the catch from the water onto land. We measure how many <b>metric tons</b> are brought ashore in each province during each quarter.</li></ul>"),
("So this is different from locations?","<ul><li>Yes. \u2018Municipal\u2019 describes the <b>type of fishing operation</b> (no boat or a small boat). It does not mean our records are grouped by municipality.</li><li>Our records are grouped by <b>province</b>, the location being analyzed.</li></ul>"),
("How can you ensure this dataset only accounts for small-scale fisheries?","<ul><li>We use PSA\u2019s dataset specifically labeled <b>Marine Municipal Fisheries</b> (no boat, or boats of 3 gross tons or less), selected separately from commercial fisheries and aquaculture records.</li><li>Our assurance comes from PSA\u2019s documented survey coverage and classification. Since we use published aggregate data, we do not independently verify every boat\u2019s size.</li></ul>"),
("What is the period of a quarter?","<ul><li><b>Q1</b>: January\u2013March</li><li><b>Q2</b>: April\u2013June</li><li><b>Q3</b>: July\u2013September</li><li><b>Q4</b>: October\u2013December</li></ul>"),
("What are the dependent and independent variables?","<ul><li><b>Predictor (independent variable):</b> mangrove change, tested alongside historical landings, seasonality, rainfall, SST and prior typhoon exposure.</li><li><b>Outcomes (dependent):</b> municipal fish landing shortfalls and economic exposure.</li><li>The link is <b>predictive</b>: we test whether mangrove change improves forecasts, not whether it causes shortfalls.</li></ul>"+B2)],
"bg":[
("How is your study different from Bokingkito et al. (2026)?","<table><tr><th></th><th>Bokingkito et al. (2026)</th><th>AquaTerra</th></tr><tr><td><b>Level</b></td><td>Regions</td><td>Selected provinces \u00d7 fish category</td></tr><tr><td><b>Coverage</b></td><td>Commercial + marine municipal</td><td>Marine municipal only</td></tr><tr><td><b>Output</b></td><td>Production forecasts</td><td>Forecasts \u2192 seasonal shortfalls \u2192 gross landing value at risk</td></tr><tr><td><b>Mangrove history</b></td><td>\u2014</td><td>Tested for added predictive value</td></tr><tr><td><b>Evaluation</b></td><td>Forecast accuracy</td><td>Shortfall detection and exposure rankings on unseen quarters</td></tr></table><p style=\"margin-top:10px\">Our proposed contribution is an <b>evaluated connection</b> between provincial landing forecasts, seasonal shortfalls and economic planning information. Their work is our foundation.</p>"),
("How do you define mangrove change in this study?","<ul><li>Historical <b>increases or decreases in the area covered by mangroves</b> in the selected coastal provinces.</li><li>We examine whether this habitat history provides useful information for predicting municipal fish landings, alongside historical landings and other environmental predictors. The relationship is <b>tested rather than assumed</b>.</li></ul>")],
"obj":[
("What data are you examining, and what are you measuring?","<table><tr><th>Question</th><th>What is measured</th></tr><tr><td><b>RQ1 Benchmarks</b></td><td>Historical landing volumes (mt) by province, fish category and quarter.</td></tr><tr><td><b>RQ2 Predictive relationship</b></td><td>Inputs (historical landings & seasonality, mangrove history, prior typhoon exposure, rainfall, SST) \u2192 next-quarter landing volume (mt). MAE, RMSE, MASE vs the seasonal baseline. Tests <b>predictive usefulness, not causation</b>.</td></tr><tr><td><b>RQ3 Valuation</b></td><td>Projected shortfall = positive (benchmark \u2212 forecast). Gross landing value at risk = shortfall \u00d7 historical reference price (\u20b1). A valuation calculation, not a relationship test.</td></tr><tr><td><b>RQ4 Unseen quarters</b></td><td>Precision & recall of shortfall periods; ranking agreement with observed landings; price sensitivity.</td></tr></table>"),
("What are your study's expected values?","<ul><li><b>Landing forecast</b>: continuous, next-quarter metric tons</li><li><b>Projected shortfall</b>: continuous, metric tons below the seasonal benchmark</li><li><b>Economic exposure</b>: continuous, gross landing value in pesos</li><li><b>Shortfall indicator</b>: categorical, shortfall projected or not</li><li><b>Exposure ranking</b>: ordinal, province\u2013fish combinations by exposure</li><li><b>Evaluation results</b>: accuracy, shortfall detection, ranking agreement, price sensitivity</li></ul>")],
"scope":[
("What does causal effects mean?","<ul><li>A causal effect means a change in one factor <b>directly produces</b> a change in another, for example claiming that mangrove loss caused fish landings to decrease.</li><li>Our study examines whether mangrove history and typhoon exposure <b>help predict</b> landings. Even if we find a relationship, that alone does not prove causation, because other factors may also explain the changes.</li></ul>"+B2),
("Which fish are currently your best options?","<ul><li>Candidate categories: <b>snapper</b> (maya-maya), <b>siganid</b> (samaral), <b>mullet</b> (kapak), <b>grouper</b> (lapu-lapu) and <b>goatfish</b> (saramulyete).</li><li>Chosen because previous studies show mangrove habitat use by species within these groups, and they are identifiable categories in the PSA dataset.</li><li>Preliminary: final selection depends on complete, compatible landing, environmental and price records.</li></ul>"),
("Which provinces are currently your best options?","<ul><li>From our preliminary inspection of PSA landing-volume records: <b>Palawan, Misamis Occidental, Negros Oriental, Tawi-Tawi, Zamboanga del Norte and Davao de Oro</b>.</li><li>They have complete quarterly volume records for the five fish categories, <b>2002 Q1 through 2026 Q3</b>.</li><li>Palawan is particularly promising: UNESCO identifies it as having the highest remaining mangrove cover in the Philippines.</li></ul>")],
"algo":[
("Why Random Forest? Are there no other notable algorithms?","<ul><li>Yes, there are. Bokingkito et al. (2026) evaluated alternatives including <b>XGBoost, Support Vector Regression and LSTM</b>, and performance differed across regions. We do not claim Random Forest is universally best.</li><li>Our justification is its role as a <b>predictor-selection tool before GAMM</b>. Nesslage et al. (2021) used Random Forest variable importance to guide more parsimonious GAMMs.</li><li>Selecting predictors with Pearson or Spearman correlations alone can miss nonlinear, nonmonotonic relationships.</li><li>We evaluate the complete workflow against a seasonal baseline on unseen quarters.</li></ul>"),
("How did Nesslage et al. (2021) formulate their algorithm?","<ol style=\"margin-left:24px\"><li><b>Two outcomes</b>: annual commercial landings (northern stock) and monthly CPUE by fishing area.</li><li><b>Matched by time and place</b>: e.g. SST and wind averaged monthly in grid cells and assigned to nearby statistical areas.</li><li><b>Lagged predictors</b>: earlier climate conditions, up to seven years.</li><li><b>Random Forest ranked predictors</b> (Boruta and Altmann importance; kept the intersection, ordered by importance). It passed on <i>which predictors</i> to examine, not its predicted landings.</li><li><b>GAMM used the predictor values</b>, with selection checks, an AR(1) residual structure and square-root-transformed landings.</li><li><b>Evaluated</b> on later observations with MAE and RMSE.</li></ol><p style=\"margin-top:8px\">What transfers to AquaTerra is the RF-guided GAMM workflow; their exact variables, lags and preprocessing are study-specific.</p>"),
("How will you separate prediction from causation?","<p>"+CAUSE+"</p>"+B2)],
"data":[
("How will you match the different datasets?","<p>We will give the records consistent location identifiers and dates, then summarize environmental information for the relevant areas and periods. We will also test appropriate lags, since earlier conditions may inform later landings. Nesslage et al. (2021) found some relationships at three- to seven-year lags; we take that as a reason to investigate delayed relationships, not to apply the same lag to every predictor.</p>"),
("How will you handle the missing data?","<p>To preserve the strong seasonality of fisheries production, we will apply <b>seasonal decomposition with Kalman smoothing</b> and <b>structural time series models with Kalman filters</b>, grounded in Benavides et al. (2023), whose study on marine fishery landings found these outperform simpler techniques for systematic, consecutive gaps. We will also use their <b>Known Sub-Sequence Algorithm (KSSA)</b> to select the lowest-error method on complete segments of our own series.</p>")],
"clients":[
("What is the main benefit to your beneficiaries?","<ul><li><b>LGUs</b>: earlier preparation of logistics. Fish is perishable and volumes swing with weather and sea conditions, so a forecast high-volume quarter could help prepare ice plants, cold storage and port logistics; a lean quarter could prompt local supply backups.</li><li><b>DTI</b>: getting ahead of supply shortages and price spikes by coordinating inter-provincial supply and giving traders and MSMEs a heads-up on expected volumes.</li><li>These are potential uses; we still need to consult these users.</li></ul>")]}
