QB=[
("Research problem & contribution",[
("Who would use these forecasts?","DTI could use the estimates when consulting with fisheries-related businesses, while LGUs and fisheries offices could use them to prioritize monitoring. We still need to consult these potential users to understand how the results would fit their actual work."),
("What makes your study different from existing forecasting research?","Our contribution is connecting the forecasts to seasonal shortfalls and their estimated gross landing value. Beyond predicting a volume, we test whether the framework can reliably identify shortfall periods and rank the province\u2013category combinations that may need attention. Bokingkito et al. (2026) already provide a Philippine environmental forecasting foundation, which we acknowledge."),
("Why forecast one quarter ahead?","PSA publishes the records quarterly, so that is the level of detail our data supports. We chose the next quarter as a near-term planning horizon, although we still need to confirm with potential users whether that timing gives them enough time to act.")]),
("Data & scope",[
("What exactly is the landing volume?","It is the weight of catch unloaded at municipal fish landing centers, measured in metric tons. It describes recorded unloadings, so we should not automatically treat it as the total amount of fish caught at sea."),
("How will you match the different datasets?","We will give the records consistent location identifiers and dates, then summarize the environmental information for the relevant areas and periods. We will also test appropriate lags, since earlier environmental conditions may provide information about later landings. Nesslage et al. (2021) found some relationships at three- to seven-year lags in golden tilefish; we take that as a reason to investigate delayed relationships, rather than applying the same lag to every predictor."),
("How will you handle missing records and reporting changes?","We will first check where the gaps occur and whether they are connected to changes in reporting or coverage. Small gaps may be suitable for carefully tested imputation, while long gaps may require shortening the period or excluding a particular province\u2013category combination. We will keep the original records and document any changes we make."),
("How do you know the environmental data represent the fishing grounds?","At this stage, provincial landing records cannot tell us exactly where every fish was caught. We will document how we link environmental areas to landing records and seek fisheries-agency advice, but we also acknowledge that this connection may be imperfect.")]),
("Methods & validation",[
("When would you issue the forecast?","We need to set a consistent issue date before the quarter being forecast begins. We will then check which records would actually be available on that date, because the most recent quarter\u2019s landing figures may not have been released yet."),
("How will you avoid using future information?","At each forecast date, we will only use records available up to that point. Predictor selection, data preparation and tuning will happen within the training periods, so later observations do not influence those decisions (Hewamalage et al., 2023)."),
("Why Random Forest and GAMM?","We use them together. Random Forest helps identify useful predictors, and GAMM uses those predictors to forecast landing volume while accounting for nonlinear relationships and differences among groups. The sequence is adapted from Nesslage et al. (2021). Our main comparison is the complete workflow against a seasonal forecasting baseline."),
("What seasonal baseline will you use?","The observed landing volume from the same quarter of the previous year. For example, the baseline forecast for Q1 is the previous year\u2019s Q1 landings. It already captures seasonality, so our more complex workflow needs to show whether it adds value."),
("How will testing reflect actual use?","We step through historical forecast dates and predict each target quarter using only earlier available information. That shows how the workflow performs across different periods instead of judging it from one forecast."),
("Which performance measures matter most?","MAE tells us how far the forecasts typically miss in metric tons, while RMSE highlights larger mistakes. MASE helps compare errors across differently sized series. We also check how often shortfall alerts are correct, how many actual shortfalls are missed, and whether the monetary rankings are reliable."),
("What improvement would you consider useful?","We have not set a numerical threshold yet. We need to consider whether the improvement is consistent and meaningful for users, especially when balancing missed shortfalls against false alerts. If the workflow adds little value over the seasonal baseline, we would not recommend it for operational use."),
("What if overall results hide poor performance in some areas?","We will break the results down by province, fish category and quarter, showing where the workflow performs well and where users should be more cautious, rather than relying on one overall accuracy figure.")]),
("Environmental interpretation",[
("How will you separate prediction from causation?",CAUSE),
("What does an important environmental variable tell you?","It tells us that the variable provided useful information to the fitted model. We would still check its stability and its relationship with other predictors before interpreting it, and importance alone would not establish causation."),
("Could environmental averages hide important details?","Yes. A quarterly average could hide a short extreme event, and a provincial average could hide differences between coastal areas. One refinement is to test whether a small set of alternative, justified summaries changes the results, with those choices evaluated within training data."),
("What if a relationship goes against established fisheries knowledge?","We would first check whether it comes from a data problem, an unsuitable lag, geographic mismatch, or correlation with another predictor. We would also seek domain advice and check whether the pattern appears consistently before giving it an ecological interpretation.")]),
("Value at risk & dashboard",[
("How will you calculate gross landing value at risk?","We subtract the forecast volume from the historical seasonal benchmark. If that difference is positive, it becomes the projected shortfall. We then multiply the shortfall by a compatible historical reference price, making sure the units match. The exact benchmark calculation and historical window still need to be finalized."),
("Which historical prices will you use?","We prioritize compatible first-sale prices for the same province and fish category. Where those are unavailable, we may calculate historical unit-value proxies from matching marine municipal production-value and volume records. We will test alternative reference prices to see how much they change the estimates and rankings. PSA\u2019s general farmgate-price table needs a coverage check before we apply it to marine municipal records."),
("What does \u201cat risk\u201d mean here?","It means the historical-price value associated with a projected landing shortfall. It is a planning estimate, so users should not interpret it as confirmed lost profit, household income loss or disaster damage."),
("How will you show uncertainty?","The exact method is not finalized yet. A useful addition would be a prediction range around each forecast, with its coverage checked on held-out observations. We would show alternative-price scenarios separately, because uncertainty in landing volume and sensitivity to price assumptions are different things."),
("What decisions could the dashboard support?","It could help users decide which areas to monitor more closely and where to begin discussions about sourcing, deliveries or possible supply gaps. Decisions about assistance eligibility, fishing restrictions or confirmed financial losses would need additional evidence."),
("How will you know whether users understand the dashboard?","We plan to seek agency feedback on its usefulness. One refinement is to ask users to interpret sample results and explain what action they would consider, which helps identify confusing labels or misleading interpretations. The participants and assessment procedure still need to be specified.")]),
("Limitations & pressure test",[
("What is the biggest threat to your findings?","Whether the datasets can be matched meaningfully. If the environmental summaries represent the wrong areas or the prices do not match the fisheries coverage, a sophisticated model would still be learning from poorly matched information."),
("What if reports are missing during low landings or severe weather?","That could bias the results, because the missing periods may be exactly the difficult situations we want to forecast. We need to examine when gaps occur and recognize that filling them automatically could hide real declines or make performance look better than it is."),
("Could other factors explain the environmental patterns?","Yes. Fishing effort, demand, regulations, fuel costs and reporting practices could also affect recorded landings. Where we cannot include suitable records for those factors, we will acknowledge them as alternative explanations."),
("How will you show that improvements are reliable?","We keep selection and tuning inside training data and check performance across different chronological evaluation periods where feasible. We also limit model complexity and remember that our pooled rows share environmental histories, so they are not all independent observations (Hewamalage et al., 2023)."),
("What if the workflow does not outperform the baseline?","Then that is what we report. The study would still show whether the proposed predictors and workflow add value under our data conditions, and it could establish that a simpler seasonal method is the better option for the evaluated setting."),
("What conclusion should agencies avoid?","They should avoid saying that our forecast proves mangrove change or a typhoon caused a particular monetary loss. Our results estimate future landings and benchmark-relative value, without establishing that causal connection."),
("What would you improve first with another year?","We would prioritize information linking landing records to actual fishing grounds and fishing effort. That would give a stronger basis for matching environmental conditions and understanding why recorded landings change.")]),
("Added answers",[
("How will you handle the missing data? (method)","To preserve strong seasonality, we will apply seasonal decomposition with Kalman smoothing and structural time series models with Kalman filters, grounded in Benavides et al. (2023), whose study on marine fishery landings found these outperform simpler techniques for systematic, consecutive gaps. We will also use their Known Sub-Sequence Algorithm (KSSA) to select the lowest-error method on complete segments of our own series."),
("What is the main benefit to your beneficiaries?","For LGUs, earlier preparation of logistics and less spoilage: knowing when a high-volume quarter is coming could help prepare ice plants, cold storage and port logistics; a lean quarter could prompt local supply backups. For DTI, getting ahead of supply shortages and price spikes by coordinating inter-provincial supply and giving traders and MSMEs a heads-up. These are potential uses that still need consultation.")])]
